 the plant and carries water. Petals and seeds have different jobs."}, "In \u201ca wooden chair\u201d, which word names the material?": {"id": "science-object-material", "type": "classify", "prompt": "In \u201ca wooden chair\u201d, which word names the material?", "options": [{"id": "wood", "label": "Wood", "emoji": "\ud83e\udeb5"}, {"id": "chair", "label": "Chair", "emoji": "\ud83e\ude91"}, {"id": "sit", "label": "Sit", "emoji": "\ud83e\uddcd"}], "answer": "wood", "explanation": "A chair is an object. Wood is the material it can be made from.", "wrongFeedback": "The object is the chair. The material is wood."}, "Which material is usually useful for keeping rain out of a raincoat?": {"id": "science-material-waterproof", "type": "classify", "prompt": "Which material is usually useful for keeping rain out of a raincoat?", "options": [{"id": "plastic", "label": "Plastic", "emoji": "\ud83e\udde5"}, {"id": "paper", "label": "Paper", "emoji": "\ud83d\udcc4"}, {"id": "sponge", "label": "Sponge", "emoji": "\ud83e\uddfd"}], "answer": "plastic", "explanation": "Plastic can be waterproof, so it can help keep rain out. The exact material and design still matter.", "wrongFeedback": "A raincoat needs a material that keeps water out. Plastic can be waterproof."}, "Which material is usually absorbent for wiping up a small spill?": {"id": "science-material-absorbent", "type": "classify", "prompt": "Which material is usually absorbent for wiping up a small spill?", "options": [{"id": "paper", "label": "Paper", "emoji": "\ud83d\udcc4"}, {"id": "glass", "label": "Glass", "emoji": "\ud83e\ude9f"}, {"id": "metal", "label": "Metal", "emoji": "\ud83e\udd44"}], "answer": "paper", "explanation": "Paper can absorb water. Glass and metal are usually not absorbent.", "wrongFeedback": "Absorbent materials soak up liquid. Paper is the best choice here."}, "Which record could help us compare weather each day?": {"id": "science-season-record", "type": "classify", "prompt": "Which record could help us compare weather each day?", "options": [{"id": "weather-chart", "label": "A weather chart", "emoji": "\ud83d\udcca"}, {"id": "single-guess", "label": "One guess", "emoji": "\ud83c\udfb2"}, {"id": "story-only", "label": "A story with no dates", "emoji": "\ud83d\udcd6"}], "answer": "weather-chart", "explanation": "A dated weather chart lets us compare observations across days.", "wrongFeedback": "To compare weather, record observations with dates in a chart or table."}, "Which change might we observe as spring begins?": {"id": "science-season-spring", "type": "classify", "prompt": "Which change might we observe as spring begins?", "options": [{"id": "buds-open", "label": "Buds opening", "emoji": "\ud83c\udf31"}, {"id": "always-snow", "label": "Snow every day", "emoji": "\u2744\ufe0f"}, {"id": "no-daylight", "label": "No daylight", "emoji": "\ud83c\udf11"}], "answer": "buds-open", "explanation": "Plants may grow new leaves or flowers in spring. Weather varies, so this is an observation to check, not a promise for every day.", "wrongFeedback": "Buds opening can be a spring change. Keep seasonal statements as observations, because weather varies."}, "Which is a question a scientist could investigate?": {"id": "science-enquiry-question", "type": "classify", "prompt": "Which is a question a scientist could investigate?", "options": [{"id": "paper-absorb", "label": "Which paper absorbs more water?", "emoji": "\ud83d\udca7"}, {"id": "best-ever", "label": "What is the best thing ever?", "emoji": "\ud83c\udfc6"}, {"id": "magic-answer", "label": "Can magic answer it?", "emoji": "\u2728"}], "answer": "paper-absorb", "explanation": "A simple question can be tested by observing and recording what happens.", "wrongFeedback": "Choose a question that can be explored with an observation or simple test."}, "To compare two paper towels fairly, what should we try to keep the same?": {"id": "science-fair-test", "type": "classify", "prompt": "To compare two paper towels fairly, what should we try to keep the same?", "options": [{"id": "same-water", "label": "The amount of water", "emoji": "\ud83d\udca7"}, {"id": "different-everything", "label": "Everything different", "emoji": "\ud83c\udfb2"}, {"id": "no-record", "label": "No record", "emoji": "\ud83d\ude48"}], "answer": "same-water", "explanation": "Keeping the amount of water the same makes the comparison fairer. We can change one thing and observe the result.", "wrongFeedback": "Keep important conditions the same so the comparison is fair."}, "Which tool can help us observe a small leaf closely?": {"id": "science-observe-tool", "type": "classify", "prompt": "Which tool can help us observe a small leaf closely?", "options": [{"id": "hand-lens", "label": "Hand lens", "emoji": "\ud83d\udd0e"}, {"id": "pillow", "label": "Pillow", "emoji": "\ud83d\udecf\ufe0f"}, {"id": "whistle", "label": "Whistle", "emoji": "\ud83d\udcef"}], "answer": "hand-lens", "explanation": "A hand lens magnifies small details so we can observe closely.", "wrongFeedback": "A hand lens helps us see small details."}, "Which way could we record how many red and green leaves we found?": {"id": "science-record-data", "type": "classify", "prompt": "Which way could we record how many red and green leaves we found?", "options": [{"id": "tally-table", "label": "A tally table", "emoji": "\ud83d\udccb"}, {"id": "secret-guess", "label": "A secret guess", "emoji": "\ud83e\udd2b"}, {"id": "blank-page", "label": "A blank page", "emoji": "\ud83d\udcc4"}], "answer": "tally-table", "explanation": "A tally table helps us count and compare groups of observations.", "wrongFeedback": "Use a table or tally to record and compare observations."}, "Predict: which object will make the longest shadow near a lamp, a small bead or a tall block?": {"id": "science-observe-conclusion", "type": "investigation", "prompt": "Predict: which object will make the longest shadow near a lamp, a small bead or a tall block?", "predictions": [{"id": "tall-block", "label": "The tall block", "emoji": "\ud83e\uddf1"}, {"id": "small-bead", "label": "The small bead", "emoji": "\u26aa"}], "observation": "With the objects the same distance from the lamp, the tall block made the longer shadow.", "options": [{"id": "block-supported", "label": "The tall block made the longer shadow", "emoji": "\ud83e\uddf1"}, {"id": "bead-supported", "label": "The bead made the longer shadow", "emoji": "\u26aa"}, {"id": "not-recorded", "label": "There was no observation", "emoji": "\ud83d\ude48"}], "answer": "block-supported", "explanation": "We made a prediction, observed the test and used the result to answer the question.", "wrongFeedback": "Use the observation: the tall block made the longer shadow in this test."}, "Predict: in winter, will daylight usually last longer or shorter than in summer?": {"id": "science-season-daylight", "type": "investigation", "prompt": "Predict: in winter, will daylight usually last longer or shorter than in summer?", "predictions": [{"id": "winter-shorter", "label": "Usually shorter in winter", "emoji": "\ud83c\udf19"}, {"id": "winter-longer", "label": "Usually longer in winter", "emoji": "\u2600\ufe0f"}], "observation": "Our daylight record shows 8 hours on a winter day and 16 hours on a summer day.", "options": [{"id": "shorter-supported", "label": "The winter day was shorter", "emoji": "\ud83c\udf19"}, {"id": "longer-supported", "label": "The winter day was longer", "emoji": "\u2600\ufe0f"}, {"id": "not-comparable", "label": "The record cannot compare them", "emoji": "\ud83e\udd14"}], "answer": "shorter-supported", "explanation": "The record supports the prediction that this winter day had fewer daylight hours than this summer day. Records help us compare observations.", "wrongFeedback": "Read the record: 8 winter hours is fewer than 16 summer hours."}, "Which body part is linked with hearing?": {"id": "science-sense-ears", "type": "classify", "prompt": "Which body part is linked with hearing?", "options": [{"id": "ears"